A1. Cracks wise. ~ JESTS
A5. This fellow gets you hot and bothered. ~ DEVIL
A9. And this fellow is the captain of his sole. ~ COOK
A11. This covers a lot of ground. ~ AREA
A12. Chinese house cat. ~ DRAGON
A14. This has a steady job up above. ~ HARPER
A16. This will measure your length in Chicago. ~ GAT
A17. An Indian head. ~ NABOB
A19. This usually leaves in the spring. ~ OAK
A20. A famous comeback. ~ ECHO
A22. John Sumner is a bird of this. ~ PRY
A23. What dilettantes dilly at. ~ ARTS
A24. These make tap-dancers tap. ~ KEGS
A26. You won't make a hit if you do this. ~ MISS
A27. Dirty dogs. ~ ROTTERS
A28. This is hard to fathom. ~ DEEP
A30. A fraternity persuader. ~ SLAT
A33. Prejudice. ~ BIAS
A34. All over but the shooting. ~ WED
A36. God save the King here. ~ ERIN
A38. Measures printers resort to. Dora thinks these are things that give shade. ~ EMS
A39. Take 'em or leave 'em. ~ DARES
A41. A good place to go for a drive. ~ TEE
A42. This is usually left at the post. ~ LETTER
A44. A swell front. Dora thinks this a Kelly-Springfield. ~ ATTIRE
A46. To buy low and sell high. ~ EARN
A47. Your friends won't tell who these are. ~ FOES
A48. How to heat up a ukelele. ~ STRUM
A49. Good currents. ~ WATTS

D1. A bench-warmer. ~ JUDGE
D2. To burn up. ~ SCATHE
D3. A glad rag. ~ TOG
D4. In the near future. ~ SOON
D5. A kind of olive. ~ DRAB
D6. A long time for a poet. ~ EER
D7. What your nose knows. ~ VAPORS
D8. What the tramp and the old lady went into the woods for. ~ LARKS
D10. A kind of sack. ~ KNAP
D11. A good cry at sea. ~ AHOY
D13. This meant a good stretch in old Spain. ~ RACK
D15. Groceries. ~ EATS
D18. Cain was the first one. ~ BROTHER
D21. Fairy-tale accessories. ~ OGRES
D23. This is hard to get seated on. ~ AISLE
D25. Dunk. ~ SOP
D26. Pseudonym for every good little Lucy Stoner. ~ MRS
D28. Rockefeller's dividend. ~ DIME
D29. The big parade. ~ EASTER
D31. A member of the unemployed. ~ ARTIST
D32. It costs money to shed this. ~ TIER
D33. Waist material (pl.) ~ BELTS
D34. What husbands do to their best gentlemen friends. ~ WARN
D35. A good way to go to the talkies. ~ DEAF
D37. We all have a lot of these. ~ NEEDS
D39. Hide. ~ DERM
D40. What sailors do to their chow. ~ STOW
D43. This is Greek to us. ~ TAU
D45. London waterworks. ~ TEA


